  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 326 AM EDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nA wetter pattern looks likely this weekend as mid-level ridging over \nthe Gulf is forecast to break down, giving way to a more zonal flow \npattern aloft across the Florida peninsula. At the same time, an \nupper level shortwave feature will begin to develop over the western \nGulf. Moisture will pool across the area on Saturday ahead of this \nfeature, and with ridging aloft and near the surface both weakening, \nweaker northeasterly winds will allow sea breeze dynamics to once\nagain play a more prominent role in convective development during\nthe afternoon. This will mean a gradual increase in rain chances \nthrough the day, with the majority of convection focused across \ninterior and southwest FL during the afternoon hours where the \nbreezes meet. Afternoon temperatures could peak in the upper 80s. \n\nConditions become more unsettled Sunday into Monday as the \naforementioned upper-level disturbance approaches the peninsula. \nThis feature will help support large-scale ascent across the area, \nand should cool the mid-levels enough to support more instability \nalong the atmospheric column. This is supported by the ECMWF Extreme \nForecast Index (EFI) from yesterday's 12Z run, which shows the \nensemble leaning towards unusually high CAPE across the area.As \nsuch, expect scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms each \nafternoon. Frequent lightning, gusty winds and locally heavy \nrainfall will be the main concerns with any of the stronger storms\nthat develop. \n\nConditions look to dry out as we head into the middle of next week, \nwith chances for rainfall limited to some coastal showers each day \n(PoPs 15-20%). Temperatures will cool down a bit Sunday-Monday with \nthe increased storminess and cloud cover, with highs in the mid 80s. \nLows will slide into the mid 60s across the interior, and the low\n70s along the coasts.",
